<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tavira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tavira is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilao">Gilão</a> river near the Ria Formosa lagoon in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>, southern Portugal. Under Muslim rule it was a fortified port and briefly an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> in the 1140s, and in 1242 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a> under Paio Peres Correia took it for Portugal. It prospered from fishing and from trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the 15th and 16th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tavira.jpg" alt="Tavira" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Panarria · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_Tavira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
